    "reading": {
        "body": "Leading AI infrastructure companies are actively hiring for physical security and insider threat roles across data centers and manufacturing sites, with nine distinct positions posted between May and September 2026.\n\nThe concentration of these roles at infrastructure-critical companies—Nebius, OpenAI, Anthropic, Cerebras, and Crusoe—reflects a shift in how AI builders now view security. Physical access to compute hardware and manufacturing operations is no longer separable from cybersecurity. A data center intrusion or supply chain compromise poses direct operational risk to AI systems at scale.\n\nThe geographic spread tells a specific story. Nebius is hiring in both the United States and Finland, while Anthropic's role spans multiple US cities. Cerebras and Crusoe are anchored in manufacturing hubs. These positions address a real constraint: as AI companies operate their own infrastructure or oversee colocation environments, they must protect the buildings and power systems that compute depends on. Insider threat detection is the corresponding layer, securing access from within.\n\nThe data does not reveal hiring volume trends over time, competitive intensity for these roles, or whether these openings reflect newly created positions or turnover. It also does not cover what specific threats these companies anticipate or how their physical security strategies differ from traditional data center operators.",
